<h1>Vertical Lift &amp; Box Elevators</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A vertical lift conveyor moves cases, cartons, totes, and other discrete products straight up or down between different floor levels or equipment elevations, using a fraction of the floor space that incline conveyors require. Continuous vertical lifts (CVLs) circulate multiple platforms in a loop for high-throughput applications, while reciprocating lifts (VRCs) shuttle a single platform between levels for lower-volume needs. Ryson, Ambaflex, Verticon, and TKF manufacture vertical lift conveyors and box elevators for packaging, distribution, and end-of-line applications.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Vertical Lift</h2>
<p>Packaging lines frequently need to move product between mezzanine levels, over obstructions, or from processing equipment at one height to packaging equipment at another. <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> solve this but consume significant floor space. A 10-foot elevation change at a 20-degree incline requires 27 feet of horizontal run. At a 30-degree incline, it still requires 17 feet. In tight production facilities, that floor space does not exist.</p>
<p>Vertical lift conveyors eliminate the horizontal run entirely. The product moves straight up within a 4x4 to 6x6 foot footprint regardless of the elevation change, whether 5 feet or 30 feet. This saves 60-90% of the floor space compared to incline conveyors. The floor space savings often justify the higher equipment cost of vertical lifts, especially in facilities where every square foot of production floor has value.</p>
<p>Vertical lifts also maintain product orientation. Incline conveyors can shift, tilt, or rotate products during the climb. Vertical lifts carry products on flat platforms that keep the product level and oriented correctly for downstream equipment, critical for <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a>, <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating</a>, and case packing operations where product orientation matters.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Vertical Lift Types</h2>
<h3>Continuous Vertical Lift (CVL)</h3>
<p>A continuous vertical lift circulates multiple platforms attached to two parallel chains in a continuous loop. Platforms ascend on one side of the machine and descend on the other, simultaneously. Product loads onto a platform at the bottom (or top for lowering), rides the platform up, and transfers off at the discharge level. The next platform arrives immediately, so there is no waiting for a return cycle.</p>
<p>CVLs deliver the highest throughput of any vertical conveyor type, 30 to 60+ cases per minute depending on platform spacing and chain speed. Verticon and Ryson manufacture CVLs for packaging and distribution applications. Platform sizes range from 12x12 inches (small cartons) to 48x48 inches (full pallet loads on heavy-duty models). The platforms remain level throughout the loop, keeping product stable and oriented.</p>
<p>CVLs require infeed and outfeed conveyors that synchronize with the moving platforms. The product must transfer onto the platform during the brief window as the platform passes the load point. Timing conveyors or <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyors</a> at the infeed meter products to match the platform arrival rate. Misaligned timing causes product jams or missed platforms.</p>
<h3>C-Frame Elevator</h3>
<p>A C-frame elevator uses platforms attached to a chain loop that follows a C-shaped path, ascending vertically, traversing horizontally at the top, and descending vertically on the other side. The C-path allows the elevator to load on one side and discharge on the opposite side at a different elevation, creating a pass-through configuration. This is useful when the infeed and outfeed conveyors approach from opposite directions.</p>
<p>C-frame elevators share the continuous operation advantage of CVLs, platforms circulate without stopping. Throughput ranges from 20 to 50 units per minute. The horizontal traverse at the top of the C-path adds width to the machine footprint compared to a straight vertical CVL, but the pass-through layout simplifies conveyor routing in facilities where conveyors approach from opposite sides of the elevation change.</p>
<h3>Z-Frame Conveyor</h3>
<p>A Z-frame conveyor follows a Z-shaped path: horizontal infeed, steep incline (60-80 degrees), and horizontal outfeed at the elevated level. The Z-path uses a single continuous belt with cleats or grippers that hold product during the steep climb. Z-frame conveyors bridge the gap between incline conveyors and true vertical lifts, steeper than a standard incline but with horizontal sections for easy loading and unloading.</p>
<p>Z-frame conveyors handle cases, cartons, bags, and pouches. The product rides on the belt surface between cleats during the incline section. Belt speed through the incline section is typically 50-150 FPM. Z-frame conveyors cost less than CVLs and C-frame elevators because they use a single belt drive rather than a chain-and-platform system. Dorner, mk North America, and QC Industries offer Z-frame configurations in their belt conveyor product lines.</p>
<p>The horizontal infeed and outfeed sections of the Z-frame simplify integration with upstream and downstream conveyors. Product transfers onto the flat infeed section at normal belt speed, then the belt carries it through the steep section without requiring platform synchronization. This makes Z-frames easier to integrate than CVLs for moderate-throughput applications (10-30 cases per minute).</p>
<h3>Vertical Reciprocating Conveyor (VRC)</h3>
<p>A VRC uses a single platform that shuttles up and down between two or more levels. The platform loads at one level, travels to the target level, stops, unloads, and returns. Unlike CVLs, VRCs stop at each level, cycle time includes travel time plus load/unload time. Throughput is lower (5-15 cycles per minute) but the mechanical simplicity and lower cost make VRCs suitable for lower-volume applications.</p>
<p>VRCs are classified as conveyors, not elevators, under ASME B20.1, provided they carry only materials, not people. This distinction matters because conveyors face different code requirements than elevators (no pit, no hoistway enclosure in many jurisdictions). PFlow, Wildeck, and Autoquip manufacture VRCs for packaging and distribution facilities. Platform capacities range from 500 lbs to 6,000 lbs, handling everything from single cartons to full pallets.</p>
<p>For <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">detailed VRC specifications</a>, including code requirements and load capacity ratings, see the dedicated VRC page. VRCs are the most common vertical conveyor type for low-to-moderate throughput packaging lines because of their lower cost, simpler controls, and straightforward maintenance.</p>
<h3>Spiral Conveyors</h3>
<p>Spiral conveyors wrap a continuous belt or slat chain in a helical path around a central drum, moving product up or down at a controlled rate. Spirals combine continuous operation (no stopping) with gentle handling, product rides on a flat surface with no platform transfers. Ryson and Ambaflex dominate the packaging spiral conveyor market.</p>
<p>Spirals handle 20 to 200+ units per minute depending on product size and spiral diameter. Elevation changes from 5 to 30+ feet are common. Spiral conveyors use more floor space than CVLs (the spiral diameter is 5-10 feet) but less than incline conveyors. They excel at <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation during elevation changes</a>, product can queue on the spiral without backpressure if the downstream line stops.</p>
