<h2 id="integration-heading">Packaging Line Integration</h2>
<h3>Infeed Conveyor Requirements</h3>
<p>Continuous vertical lifts (CVLs and C-frames) require precise product timing at the infeed. Product must arrive at the load point exactly when an empty platform passes. A timing conveyor or servo-driven <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> upstream of the lift creates the required product spacing and timing. Photoeye sensors detect platform position and signal the infeed conveyor to release the next product.</p>
<p>VRCs have simpler infeed requirements because the platform stops at the load level. A standard transport conveyor pushes product onto the stopped platform. A sensor confirms product presence, and the lift begins its travel. No synchronization is needed.</p>
<h3>Outfeed and Downstream Handling</h3>
<p>Product discharging from a vertical lift must transfer smoothly onto the outfeed conveyor. CVLs and spirals deliver product continuously, the outfeed conveyor must run at matching speed to prevent product accumulation at the discharge point. VRCs deliver product in batches (one platform load at a time), the outfeed conveyor clears the platform before the lift can return. An <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a> downstream of a VRC absorbs the intermittent delivery pattern.</p>
<h3>Controls Integration</h3>
<p>Vertical lift controls must communicate with the overall line PLC to coordinate start/stop, speed changes, and fault handling. Most lift manufacturers supply the lift with its own control panel that interfaces to the line PLC via discrete I/O (start, stop, fault, platform-at-level signals) or Ethernet/IP for more detailed data exchange. The lift controller handles platform positioning, chain speed, and safety interlocks internally. The line PLC handles product flow logic, infeed timing, and outfeed coordination.</p>
<p>Safety interlocks include platform position sensors, overtravel limits, chain break detectors, and access door switches. Opening an access door stops the lift. A chain break detector stops the drive immediately if chain tension drops, preventing a platform from falling. ASME B20.1 and local building codes dictate the required safety devices for vertical conveyors in packaging facilities.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Vertical Lift Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Mezzanine level changes:</strong> Moving cases from ground-floor packaging to mezzanine-level palletizing, or from mezzanine production to ground-floor shipping. CVLs and spirals handle the continuous flow at 30-60+ cases per minute. VRCs handle lower-volume mezzanine transfers.</p>
<p><strong>Over-conveyor bridges:</strong> Lifting product over a perpendicular conveyor line, walkway, or forklift aisle, then lowering it on the other side. A pair of vertical lifts (up and down) with a horizontal conveyor bridge creates an overhead pass-through that maintains floor-level traffic flow.</p>
<p><strong>End-of-line case elevation:</strong> Sealed cases from a <a href="/conveyor-functions/slug-loading/">case packer</a> elevate to palletizer infeed height. The palletizer may sit on a raised platform to allow pallet removal by forklift at floor level. A CVL or spiral provides continuous case flow to the palletizer without the long incline run that an incline conveyor requires.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-level distribution:</strong> Distribution centers with pick modules on multiple mezzanine levels use vertical lifts to move totes and cases between levels. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> on each level feed product to the lift, which delivers it to the main shipping sortation level.</p>
<p><strong>Bulk product elevation:</strong> For granular, loose, and bulk products that cannot ride on a lift platform, <a href="/conveyor-types/flighted-scoop-conveyors/">flighted and scoop conveyors</a> provide the vertical elevation function with enclosed product retention.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Vertical Lift</h2>
<p>Match the lift type to these parameters:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Throughput.</strong> CVLs and spirals for 30+ UPM. Z-frames for 10-30 UPM. VRCs for under 15 cycles per minute. Throughput is the primary selection driver.</li>
<li><strong>Elevation change.</strong> All types handle 5-30+ feet. VRCs handle the highest elevations (up to 50+ feet) because the single-platform design scales vertically without adding complexity. Spirals become very tall and expensive beyond 30 feet.</li>
<li><strong>Product weight and size.</strong> CVLs and spirals handle individual cases and cartons (typically under 100 lbs). VRCs handle pallets and heavy loads (up to 6,000 lbs). Z-frames handle individual cases and cartons (under 50 lbs).</li>
<li><strong>Floor space.</strong> CVLs use the least floor space for vertical-only elevation. Z-frames use more because of the horizontal infeed/outfeed sections. Spirals use the most floor space because of the helix diameter.</li>
<li><strong>Budget.</strong> VRCs and Z-frames cost $$ to $$$. CVLs, C-frames, and spirals cost $$$ to $$$$. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for factors that move projects up and down the cost scale.</li>
</ol>
