<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a vertical lift conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A vertical lift conveyor moves products straight up or down between elevations using platforms, carriages, or opposing belts in a true vertical path. Types include continuous vertical lifts (CVLs), C-frame elevators, Z-frame conveyors, VRCs, and spirals. They save 60-90% of floor space compared to incline conveyors.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">CVL vs. VRC, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>CVLs circulate multiple platforms continuously for high throughput (30-60+ UPM). VRCs shuttle a single platform up and down with stops at each level for lower throughput (5-15 cycles/min). CVLs cost more but handle higher volumes. VRCs are simpler, cheaper, and handle heavier loads including full pallets.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How much floor space does a vertical lift require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most packaging-line vertical lifts need 4x4 to 6x6 feet of floor space. A comparable incline conveyor needs 15-40 feet. Spirals need 5-10 feet in diameter. The vertical lift footprint stays constant regardless of elevation change height.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What products can vertical lifts handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Cases, cartons, totes, trays, bundles, and pallets. Products need a stable flat bottom. Weight ranges from 1 lb (small cartons on CVLs) to 6,000 lbs (pallets on heavy-duty VRCs). Loose, granular, or bulk products need <a href="/conveyor-types/flighted-scoop-conveyors/">flighted or scoop conveyors</a> instead.</p>

</div>
